1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,660
There's a shipwreck about 30 feet down,

2
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:05,044
off the beach at Indiana Dunes State Park.

3
00:00:05,594 --> 00:00:07,964
She's the steamer Marshall, a wooden ship

4
00:00:07,974 --> 00:00:10,174
built in 1891 to haul lumber.

5
00:00:10,404 --> 00:00:13,315
Around 1910, she was rebuilt to pump sand

6
00:00:13,325 --> 00:00:14,389
from the lake bottom.

7
00:00:14,839 --> 00:00:16,864
That June, anchored off the dunes near

8
00:00:16,874 --> 00:00:18,510
Chesterton, she sprang a leak.

9
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:19,773
The crew patched it.

10
00:00:20,223 --> 00:00:23,686
Then, early on June 11, 1911, a squall

11
00:00:23,696 --> 00:00:24,878
blew in from the west.

12
00:00:25,108 --> 00:00:26,979
The patch failed, and the ship rolled

13
00:00:26,989 --> 00:00:27,344
over.

14
00:00:27,994 --> 00:00:29,364
Four of her crew were lost.

15
00:00:30,114 --> 00:00:32,329
Decades later, salvagers raised the wreck

16
00:00:32,339 --> 00:00:33,760
and tried to haul it away.

17
00:00:33,990 --> 00:00:35,690
It ended up back on the bottom.

18
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:38,287
Her propeller now sits beside the park's

19
00:00:38,297 --> 00:00:39,161
beach pavilion.

20
00:00:39,391 --> 00:00:42,307
And in 2013, the wreck became Indiana's

21
00:00:42,317 --> 00:00:44,220
first underwater nature preserve.

22
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:46,832
Comment your town for the next La Porte

23
00:00:46,842 --> 00:00:47,573
After Dark.

